                  Calculate FRT &rarr;
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">The Electric Revolution: EV Concessions & One-for-One Scheme</h2>
            <p>The landscape of Hong Kong motoring is changing rapidly, driven almost entirely by taxation policy. If you want to bypass the most agonizing parts of the FRT, going electric is your best strategy. But it's not as simple as just buying an EV. The government has structured these incentives to actively remove internal combustion engine (ICE) vehicles from our roads.</p>
            
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Standard EV Concession:</strong> Private electric cars receive an FRT tax concession cap of up to <strong>HK$58,500</strong>. This is helpful for entry-level EVs, but if you are buying a premium electric SUV, this standard concession barely dents the massive tax bill.</li>
              <li><strong>"One-for-One Replacement" Scheme:</strong> Here is where the advanced strategy lies. The "One-for-One Replacement" scheme is the holy grail of Hong Kong car buying right now. By scrapping and deregistering an eligible old private car and replacing it with a new electric car, you receive an enhanced FRT concession cap of up to <strong>HK$172,500</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6 mb-2">Troubleshooting the One-for-One Scheme: Edge Cases</h3>
            <p>The One-for-One scheme seems brilliant on paper, but we've seen countless buyers fall into bureaucratic traps. Let's troubleshoot the most common edge cases:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Ownership Duration Trap:</strong> You must have been the registered owner of the old car for at least <em>18 consecutive months</em> immediately before deregistration. You cannot simply buy a cheap junker off Facebook Marketplace today and scrap it tomorrow for the HK$172k discount.</li>
              <li><strong>Licensing Gap Nightmare:</strong> The old vehicle must have been licensed for at least 10 months (304 days or more) within the 12 months immediately preceding deregistration. If your old car broke down, sat in a garage unlicensed for three months while you debated fixing it, and then you try to scrap it? You just lost your eligibility.</li>
              <li><strong>Name Matching:</strong> The name of the registered owner of the scrapped car must match the buyer of the new EV <em>exactly</em>. No transferring the quota to your spouse or your limited company.</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-2">Note: EV concession amounts and eligibility rules are subject to periodic review by the Environment and Ecology Bureau and Transport Department. Vehicle buyers should verify current concession caps directly via the official <a href="https://www.td.gov.hk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Transport Department EV Portal</a>.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Importing Your Dream Car: The Valuation Minefield</h2>
            <p>Let's say you want to bring in a JDM classic from Japan, a rare Porsche 911 from the UK, or just a family van that's cheaper overseas. Importing a used car is where many enthusiasts face immense frustration. The question we hear all the time is: <em>"Do I still have to pay FRT on a used car?"</em> The answer is an emphatic yes.</p>
            
            <p>The process involves submitting the imported car for valuation by the Transport Department. The TD doesn't just look at the invoice of what you paid at a Tokyo auction. They calculate the taxable value based on the estimated <em>original retail price</em> of that exact model in Hong Kong when it was brand new, and then they apply a depreciation scale based on its age.</p>
            
            <p>If you import a highly sought-after classic car that has actually appreciated in value over the last 20 years, you might find yourself fighting a complex bureaucratic battle over its "assessable value". Always hire a professional parallel import agent if you are bringing in high-value or rare vehicles; they know how to present the paperwork to the Motor Vehicles Valuation Group to optimize your tax position legally.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Annual Motor Vehicle Licensing Fees (MVT)</h2>
            <p>Once you've survived the initial onslaught of the First Registration Tax, you aren't entirely off the hook. You must pay annual vehicle license renewal fees, commonly known as road tax or Motor Vehicle Tax (MVT).</p>
            
            <p>For petrol private passenger cars, this fee is ruthlessly pegged to engine cylinder capacity (CC). It’s another reason why large V8s are rapidly becoming a dying breed in the city, while small-displacement turbocharged engines thrive.</p>

            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 font-mono text-sm">
              <li>Engine Capacity ≤ 1,500cc: HK$5,074 / year</li>
              <li>1,501cc – 2,500cc: HK$7,019 / year</li>
              <li>2,501cc – 3,500cc: HK$8,964 / year</li>
              <li>3,501cc – 4,500cc: HK$10,910 / year</li>
              <li>Above 4,500cc: HK$12,855 / year</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Transport Department Registration Procedures</h2>
            <p>Navigating the Transport Department (TD) can feel like navigating a maze. Vehicle buyers must submit registration documents (Form TD22), proof of valid third-party insurance, and a Certificate of Roadworthiness (vehicle inspection certificate for vehicles aged 6+ years). You must also settle your FRT and license fees in full at the licensing office before you receive your Vehicle Registration Document (VRD).</p>
            
            <p><strong>Pro-Tip:</strong> Never let your vehicle license expire for too long. If you renew a license after it expires, you will be charged an additional fee for the unlicensed period, plus a daily surcharge. The TD is completely unforgiving about this.</p>
            
            <p>For digital tax filing and account management tools, see our <a href="/blogs/hong-kong-etax-guide/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Hong Kong eTAX Portal Guide</a>.</p>

